        "efe53b29-1648-4fd7-a7ce-33770428d21a": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "the_title": "EVER FEEL TRAPPED IN THE STRESS-CYCLE?",
            "the_text": "<p>If you’re anxious, stressed, exhausted and struggle to sleep a lot of the time…</p><p>Chances are you have higher-than-average Cortisol (which is sometimes known as Cushing Syndrome). Fact is, yours might be out of balance COMPLETELY!</p><p>That’s because being in a chronic, long-term, low state of STRESS or ANXIETY…</p><p>…is triggering your adrenal glands to continuously release hormones, including adrenaline and cortisol.</p><p>The result is an increase in heart rate and energy as part of the “fight-or-flight” sympathetic nervous system response. It’s your body’s way of getting ready for potentially dangerous or harmful situations.</p><p>Except, when your body and adrenals stay “alert” for long periods, you encounter problems – some people call it “ADRENAL FATIGUE”. But this technically isn’t a real</p>"
          }
        },
        "fdf9501b-f13d-41f6-81c1-9b3bdcb8cbe4": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "the_title": "SO THEN, WHAT IS CORTISOL?",
            "the_text": "<p>Cortisol is known as “the stress hormone” (remember I mentioned that already?).</p><p>So to be precise, it’s a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal glands. Cortisol is VERY important for your health. It helps us out of bed each morning…</p><p>But it’s particularly important for “survival” – it can help you react when facing danger by quickly raising your heart rate and energy levels.</p><p>Cortisol helps to limit any functions that aren’t essential in a fight-or-flight situation. Once the threat passes, your hormones return to their usual levels – or they SHOULD.</p><p>However, at the end of the day, your cortisol needs to be LOWER so that you feel calm.</p>"
          }
        },
        "4df035d0-3303-4379-a6ea-d64e1d666b75": {
          "type": "text_two_blocks",
          "settings": {
            "the_title": "WHAT RAISES CORTISOL...?",
            "list_text1": "– Chronic stress",
            "list_text2": "– Depression",
            "list_text3": "– Chronic malnutrition",
            "list_text4": "– Obesity",
            "list_text5": "– Medication",
            "list_text6": "– High estrogen",
            "the_text": "<p>And too much of it can wreak havoc on your body and cause a number of unwanted symptoms – like feelings of stress, anxiety, insomnia, poor workout recovery etc., etc.The problem is PROLONGED levels of increased cortisol.\t</p>"
          }
        },
        "a958f0b8-083e-447e-a071-58be69f57af8": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "the_title": "AT TIMES, HIGH CORTISOL IS HELPFUL…<br>BUT TOO MUCH IS A BIG STRESS ON YOUR BODY!",
            "the_text": "<p>When you’re under constant stress, this response doesn’t always turn off.</p><p>This causes a cascade of hormonal and physiological responses because your body is</p><p>perceiving the stimuli (the perceived “stressor”) as a threat to your survival!</p><p><strong>So what’s the answer?</strong></p><p>Well, studies show that people who are better equipped to cope with stress, generally have a lower cortisol response to stress.</p><p>In fact, other research from the Biological Psychology Journal, shows how cortisol and stress are directly linked.</p><p>“Perceiving greater stress than usual was associated with situational elevations in cortisol”</p>"
          }
        },
        "9fd0652b-a732-44de-b5e4-933bb1044756": {
          "type": "rich_text",
          "settings": {
            "the_title": "TO COPE WITH STRESS… <br> YOU NEED TO MANAGE HOW YOUR BODY REACTS TO IT",
            "the_text": ""
          }
        }
      },
